{
  "result": "There is a lighthouse that does not guard the sea. It stands instead at the end of a quiet street, where the pavement gives up and grass takes over. Every evening, though no ships ever come that way, the keeper climbs the iron stairs and lights the lamp. He does it not from duty, but from a stubborn hope that light, offered patiently, will find someone who needs it.\n\nPeople in town have learned to time their walks by its glow. Children race home before it brightens, dogs turn their heads toward it, and old couples pause mid-argument, softened by its amber patience. On rainy nights the beam catches the rain and makes it look like falling stars, brief and bright as they strike the stones. The keeper says the light remembers every face it has ever illuminated. He keeps a small notebook by the door, though the pages are blank, because some evenings a traveler arrives asking for direction. He offers tea, points toward the road, and watches them go, grateful that his quiet place is still useful.\n\nWhen winter comes, the lighthouse wears a thin coat of frost, and the lamp hums a little louder, as if trying to keep itself awake. Travelers swear that from the top they can see not only the town below, but also a faint road leading somewhere kinder. Perhaps it is only imagination, but imagination, like lamplight, has a way of making the dark feel less final. And that is enough tonight.",
